  Is something copied that need not be? YES: the band partition at :969-997 copies every peak of the window into 12 sub-PeakMaps (plus halo duplicates). With m/z-sorted spectra, bands could be VIEWS (index ranges per spectrum per band) instead of copies — MassTraceDetection takes a whole PeakMap though; one could pass per-band filtered... MTD needs contiguous spectra. Alternative: run MTD on the full window once with more internal parallelism — but MTD is serial. So copies are the price of banding. Could instead partition CompactFrames (cheap, 10 B/peak) and materialise per band... but materialize happens per window before banding. Restructure: materialize directly into band sub-maps from compact frames (band edges computed from compact m/z sample) — avoids the double materialisation. That's a real memory+time win: eliminates wmap entirely (saves ~2× compact transient and the copy time in trace).

  Also PeakMap out copies in aggregateFrames_ (off by default). And the admission estimate.

  Question 3: MS1_TRACE 5.0x: serial band partition + serial merges + imbalance. Worth fixing? 6.1% wall → perfect fix saves ~3.5% total wall. Moderate priority, low risk (output-neutral if partition is exact — partition IS exact by design, and parallelizing the partition copy doesn't change trace sets; the canonical sort after makes order deterministic). But careful: parallelising partition appends per-spectrum per-band in different order? The partition loop is over spectra in order, bands independent — parallelise over bands? Each band b gets its own sub[b]; the loop over spectra appends in spectrum order — could parallelise over spectra chunks with per-chunk sub-vectors then concatenate in order. Deterministic. Medium gain (~40 s), low risk.

  PRECURSOR_INFER: serial 50.6 s, 3.7%. Parallelising the greedy loop changes seed order → output changes → hard sell. But could you parallelise per m/z-decoupled region? Isotope partners are within 5×ISO/z ≈ 5 Da; greedy marking couples across the whole intensity-sorted order. Any parallel decomposition changes results. Leave. 3.7% not worth it. State plainly.

  Question 4: diaTracer 3.1× cheaper CPU. Algorithmic differences:
  1. Frame summing before peak picking (diaTracer core): sums ~5 adjacent cycles into composite (m/z,IM) frames → 5× fewer point observations to trace, and higher S/N means the noise threshold kills more junk → dramatically fewer fragment traces. speXtract traces RAW frames at noise 10 → 8.5M traces/window. Trace count drives BOTH trace-stage and score-stage work superlinearly (score work ∝ precursors × candidate fragments).
  2. No MassTraceDetection-equivalent greedy m/z tracing: diaTracer extracts fragment peaks from the summed matrix directly (RF peak picking) and correlates XICs — skips the per-apex candidate scan whose cost is superlinear in band density (the code comment at :2381-2383 admits MTD's per-apex candidate scan is superlinear: "halving a band halves the candidate list for every apex" — i.e. work ∝ N²/D... the very fact that banding reduces TOTAL CPU 11,168→2,972 s across 5→24 bands proves the tracing algorithm does redundant quadratic-ish work).
  3. Candidate gating before correlation: diaTracer correlates only fragments passing its apex filters against the precursor's own elution window (few points); speXtract scores every IM-band fragment against every precursor with support-length dot products, over 932k precursors including ~40% guessed singletons that mostly emit nothing (require_isotope_support doc: guessed spectra identify at 1.3%... but they still get SCORED). 40% of scoring work is on precursors that diaTracer arguably never generates? Actually diaTracer does assign default charges too. But: speXtract emits per precursor HYPOTHESIS with fan-out 6.45 → the score loop covers all hypotheses.
  4. Smoothing: diaTracer smooths precursor XIC (2D Gaussian pre-filter + SG) — that REDUCES work? No, adds. But smoothing lets it use 0.3 threshold with fewer points and trust it; irrelevant to CPU.
  5. diaTracer works on the "pseudo-spectra per (precursor feature)" — fewer precursor hypotheses? We can't verify.

  The honest summary for Q4: the 3× gap is mostly (a) tracing raw vs summed frames (input volume to the tracer ~5× and superlinear tracer), and (b) the O(precursors × IM-band fragments × support) correlation volume vs diaTracer's tighter candidate generation. These are algorithmic, not tuning. But note: speXtract's share-all fan-out means it emits comparable spectrum counts, so scoring all pairs is inherent to the design (diaTracer also assigns fragments to multiple? "correlate and assign"). State plainly: the algorithm does more work by design; tuning closes maybe 30-40% of wall, not the 2.3×.

  Wait — also LOAD: speXtract spends 4,514 CPU-s on loading/picking (81.7x parallel — good), plus MS1 etc. diaTracer total CPU 26,280 s vs speXtract 82,440 s. If speXtract's window-loop CPU were cut to match work volume... The score stage could be reduced by pre-filtering candidates with a cheap RT+IM index (2D grid) so the dot product runs only on pairs passing both gates — currently the IM-band scan iterates all fragments in IM band and checks RT per element: cheap per element but × 4.7e9... The dot product only runs for pairs passing RT gate. So the expensive part (support walk) is gated. The scan itself: 39k precursors × ~IM-band-size... if IM band holds ~5-15% of 8.5M = 400k-1.2M scans per precursor = 1.6e10-4.7e10 per window × 24 — that's 4e11-1.1e12 element visits just for the RT gate scan. At ~1e9/s/core that's 400-1100 CPU-s/core... hmm × how distributed. This is plausibly the dominant score cost — the linear scan over the IM band, not the dot product! Because RT gate kills most. So a 2D (IM,RT) index (sort fragments by IM, then bucket by RT within IM blocks, or a k-d/grid) would cut score work by ~the fraction passing RT gate (delta_rt=3 s over gradient ~1800 s → ~0.3% pass) → orders-of-magnitude fewer element visits: scan cost → candidates passing both gates ≈ 400k×0.003 ≈ 1,200 per precursor. Score could drop ~10-50×. THIS is a big mitigation: spatial index for the candidate loop at :1500-1505. Output-neutral if exact (same candidate set, same dot, same gates — just enumerated faster). Must preserve determinism: candidates processed per precursor independently; order of fi within a precursor affects nothing (emission collected in frags vector, sorted later by m/z in assembleFromList_ via sortByPosition; ties in partial_sort broken by m/z then index — index is position in frags vector! Line 1664-1665: tie-break `a < b` on index into frags — enumeration order matters for exact tie-breaks. Also pslot per-precursor deterministic. So the index must yield candidates in the same fi order — sort the bucket's indices by fi. Then output-identical. Falsify cheaply: diff mzML.

  Hmm wait, one more check on score stage cost: is the IM-band scan really dominant vs dot products? Fragment support ~510 points?? The comment says up to 510 support; a fragment trace spanning the whole gradient? min_sample_rate 0.5 and min_length 0... A fragment with 510 support points passing RT gate... RT gate is apex-RT within 3 s, so support points fall in a narrow RT range but grid-walk cost = support length regardless (walks ALL support, probing pdense). If avg support ~20-40 pts and ~1,200 surviving candidates/precursor: 39k × 1,200 × 30 = 1.4e9 ops/window vs scan 1.6e10. Scan dominates ~10×. Yes, the linear IM-band scan is the score hotspot. And score = 3,753.6 window-wall ≈ ~30-40k CPU-s. Cutting scan 10× cuts score CPU maybe 7-9× → saves ~25-30k CPU-s ≈ 30% of total CPU. Wall: score window-wall 3,753.6 → maybe ~600-800 → saves ~3,000/8 ≈ 370 s wall ≈ 27% of total wall (1,351 s). Biggest single neutral win. Risk: moderate (index construction per window, memory for index, exactness of ordering). Actually simpler alternative: since fragments are sorted by IM, build per-IM-block RT-sorted sub-index, or simply sort fragments by RT within coarse IM buckets... Fine, proposal stands: bucketed 2D index.

  Also trace stage (48.7% window time, superlinear MTD): mitigations: increase bands? Measured: 12 bands optimal wall (24 bands: more threads than cores). n_conc×n_bands=96 fixed. Could go 12 windows × 8 bands? Trace work falls with more bands superlinearly (CPU 11,168→5,378→2,972 for 5/12/24) — but wall limited by threads. With 24 windows: could do n_conc=4 × 24 bands = 96: trace CPU would drop a lot (2,972 vs 5,378 on S30 = -45%)! But fewer windows in flight → worse tail/imbalance (only 4 slots for 24 skewed windows), admission memory pressure eases. The S30 measurement says flat-24-bands loses on WALL despite lower CPU "because 24 windows × 24 bands = 576 threads on 100 cores" — but with n_conc=4 × 24 = 96 threads it's NOT oversubscribed! The 576-thread scenario was n_conc=8×24=192 or the old multiplication bug. The comment says "24 windows x 24 bands = 576 threads" — that was 24 concurrent windows (old cap without division). With proper division (n_conc = 100/24 = 4), 4×24=96. Trace CPU drops 45% but score's p_team = 100/4 = 25 (more per window — fine, score parallelises over precursors well) and windows in flight 4 → memory peak halves. But outer fill worsens: 24 windows / 4 slots with 136× skew → tail risk high; heavy-first helps. Worth an A/B: (n_conc, n_bands) ∈ {(8,12), (4,24), (12,8), (6,16)}. Cheap to test via existing perf:trace_bands option (pins all windows to N bands, and n_conc auto-derives). Output-neutral (band partition is exact). Expected gain: if trace CPU halves (53.6k→~30k on this file's mix) and wall is trace-dominated... rough: window-loop CPU 77.3k → ~55k; wall 1139 → maybe 850-950 s. Uncertain; flag as experiment, medium risk (tail imbalance could eat gains).

