  Question 1 binding constraint answer: The binding constraint is the correlation kernel's raw work volume + trace stage raw work (they're 95% of window-time), NOT the concurrency cap: the grid is 96/100 threads and even a perfect scheduler only recovers the 29% idle → best-case wall ~1139×0.71≈810 s for the loop; total ~1,020 s vs diaTracer 586 s. So scheduling alone cannot close the 2.3× wall gap; work reduction must come from the algorithm side. Which numbers would have to be wrong: the stage sums (8,139.5) and window-loop CPU (77,333). If the 67.9x already reflected max achievable... The claim "29% recoverable by scheduling" fails if: (a) the admission/tail idle (972 outer-s) is actually forced by memory (admission serialized because budget < need — then it's a memory constraint, not scheduling; settle by logging admit-wait vs window count; if waits dominate, raising perf:mem_fraction or reducing per-window footprint is the fix, not scheduling); (b) if the 12× reservation model overstates (e.g., memory-bandwidth saturation: 96 threads streaming FragGrid at ~35 GB/window could be bandwidth-bound, making "idle" threads actually stalled on memory — settle with perf stat: measure memory bandwidth / stalled cycles during score stage; if bandwidth-saturated, the score index fix helps doubly by shrinking the working set).

