3,312 = 12%).

  But hold on — this assumes trace CPU really halves on S08 with 24 bands (S30 measurement) and that admission/fill behave. And 24 bands × halo on narrow windows: comment says narrow tile (19 Da) at 24 bands = 57% halo overhead — but that overhead is included in the S30 CPU measurement presumably (S30 also has 24 windows...). The per-window width-derived band heuristic was REJECTED in favour of flat 12 (:2376-2380). Flat 24 lost on WALL at 8×24=192 threads oversubscribed — but with n_conc=4 it's 96 threads. The existing option pins bands and auto-derives n_conc = 100/24 = 4. So the A/B is literally `-perf:trace_bands 24`. Also intermediate: 6×16=96. Test all.

  3. **Parallelise buildFragGrid + materialise directly into bands (skip wmap)** — fraggrid 396 s serial window-wall (~4.6% loop, and 4,354 s inner-idle capacity). Parallelise the per-fragment support indexing (lower_bounds independent per fragment; grid union via parallel merge or just build per-thread then merge). Gain: ~350 window-wall → ~45 s wall + reduced idle. Also restructure materialise→bands: saves ~2× compact transient copy time inside trace stage (part of trace's serial partition) and ~2 GB×8 memory. Output-neutral. Risk: low-medium (must preserve exact edge quantiles and CSR order — flat order = fragment order = sorted by (im,mz,rt,intensity)... wait, frag_traces sorted AFTER detectTraces_ at :2411, then buildFragGrid on sorted order. CSR entries per fragment in xic order — deterministic. Parallel build with per-fragment segments into precomputed offsets is deterministic.)

  4. **Fix the uint32_t truncation bug at :731/:742** (g.off entries cast to uint32_t despite uint64_t vector and comment claiming 64-bit) — correctness, not perf, but hostile review must flag: on the widest windows the design itself says we're within 2× of wrap; one denser file (lower ms2 noise) crosses it and every correlation silently reads wrong support spans. Zero-risk fix: remove the cast. (And g.off[i] assigned per fragment — sentinel too.) Actually also check: `g.off.resize(frags.size()+1, 0)` uint64; `supBegin returns off[i]` fine. The (uint32_t) casts truncate. Must fix. Also `int gi` grid index int — G < 2^31 fine.

  5. **Admission estimate recalibration** — 10× under-covers measured ~14× avg; raise multiplier or make it self-tuning from measured per-window RSS deltas. Prevents OOM on shared/smaller nodes; may reduce concurrency slightly here (memory headroom already allows 8). Neutral-to-none on wall for this run; risk low. Gain: robustness, and IF admission waits are found nonzero (item: measure admit-wait), then either the multiplier is too HIGH for big windows or memory-bound — measurement first: log per-window admit wait.

  6. **MS1_TRACE partition parallelisation** — 83 s wall at 5x; partition is serial; parallelise partition copies per band (order-preserving chunked append). Gain ~30-40 s wall (2-3%). Output-neutral. Low risk. Similarly PRECURSOR_INFER: leave alone (output-changing to parallelise greedy; 3.7% not worth risk).

  7. **Reduce trace work at the source (algorithmic, output-changing)**: native pre-centroid frame aggregation (trace:native_ms2_neighbors) — diaTracer's frame summing; cuts trace input volume ~5× and trace count; the code comment says tool-level aggregation lost 26% peptides, native untested/ungated. This is the one that could close the diaTracer work gap but CHANGES OUTPUT → hard sell under peptide-set gate. Mention as the honest answer to "can we reach diaTracer": only via output-changing algorithm alignment.

  8. **EPD/MTD**: trace stage 48.7% — valley-splitting (EPD) documented as adding ~38% wall when on (20:46→28:40). ms2_split_valleys default 7.0 — output-changing to disable. Not a free win.

  Also: 200 ms admission poll — replace sleep-poll with condition variable? Minor (max 200 ms granularity × few waits — negligible; but under `#pragma omp critical(admit)` while reading /proc/meminfo — every 200 ms per waiting thread, cheap. Fine, skip.)

  Also LOAD at 81.7x is fine; 55.2 s wall. Write 21 s serial — could compress? mzML write 863k spectra 21 s — fine.

  Also mention: `availableBytes_()` adds own RSS back — including the 20.8 GB compact store and 55 GB base, so budget grows with our own bloat; the 0.75 fraction of (MemAvail+RSS) means after we hold 152 GB, budget includes our own RSS → we can always admit more even as system nears exhaustion... Design intent documented (:179-180). On a dedicated node OK.

  Now, which measurement would settle open questions:
  - admit-wait time per window (distinguish scheduling tail vs memory serialization): add a timer around the for(;;) loop. Cheap.
  - per-stage CPU not just window-wall (trace vs score fill): use clock_gettime(CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID) per band/chunk, or getrusage deltas can't isolate; simplest: per-band wall times logged (already partially via span_log? No). Log per-band duration in detectTraces_ and per-chunk in score.
  - memory-bandwidth saturation during score: perf stat -e cycles,instructions,cache-misses on a score-heavy window; or run one window alone and see if score fill improves (if fill rises with fewer concurrent windows → bandwidth/contention; if unchanged → algorithmic imbalance).
  - peak RSS attribution: per-window-stage RSS logs (code has rss_() at window done only) — add RSS read at trace/grid/score stage ends for the widest window.

  Self-contradictions to flag:
  (a) The source comment at :2274-2276 claims trace=69.34%, score=28.81% of window-loop CPU (S23/S30), but THIS run's stage wall sums are trace 48.7% / score 46.1%. If the CPU split still held, trace CPU = 0.693×77,333 = 53.6k s vs max possible 12×3,965.6 = 47.6k — impossible. So either the split shifted (score became relatively heavier — plausible: different file, thresholds) or timers inconsistent. Not a contradiction of the USER's numbers per se, but shows the old 69/29 model is dead; the missing measurement is per-stage CPU.

  (b) `g.off[i] = (uint32_t)g.flat.size()` contradicts the "64-bit on purpose" comment two lines above. Latent overflow.

