  Now Q4 honest algorithmic list (3× CPU):
  1. **Input-volume to the tracer**: diaTracer sums adjacent cycles pre-centroid (S/N ↑ ~√N, point count ↓ ~N×) so its peak-picking/trace stage sees ~1/5 the points and far fewer noise traces. speXtract traces every raw frame at noise 10 → 2.19e9 peaks compacted, 8.5M traces in big windows. Trace+score are ~95% of window CPU; both scale (super)linearly with trace count. This alone can be ~2-3×.
  2. **Superlinear m/z tracer**: MTD's per-apex candidate scan makes tracing superlinear in local peak density (proven by own measurement: banding cuts TOTAL CPU 11,168→2,972 s from 5→24 bands — a linear algorithm would show flat CPU). diaTracer's matrix-extraction approach doesn't pay this.
  3. **Correlation candidate volume**: every precursor × every fragment in the IM band, linear-scanned then support-walked; ~1M precursor hypotheses incl. ~40% guessed singletons + fan-out 6.45 share-all. diaTracer correlates per detected precursor FEATURE with intensity-ranked fragment sets and retains top-500 by intensity — fewer, cheaper pairs. Also RT gating by apex distance only after full scan.
  4. **Redundant halo work**: 12 bands × 20 ppm halo + 1 Da window overlap → duplicated tracing at every edge (~12% + ~2%).
  5. Valley-splitting EPD over 8.5M traces/window (+38% wall documented) — diaTracer doesn't split OpenMS-style traces because it doesn't build them.

  Sum: plausible 3× without any "C++ vs X" implementation story.

  Also: speXtract emits 863,319 vs diaTracer 872,430 spectra — similar output, so the extra work isn't buying volume.

